        "Within Maharashtra, the pull is towards Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar, Pune and Mumbai, with Hyderabad attracting some. Others look overseas, chiefly at universities in the UK, Canada, Australia and the US. Holding a qualification that every country reads the same way keeps both options alive, and that is the most common reason a family chooses it.",
        "Indian admission offices treat an IB or IGCSE record differently from a State Board one. The Association of Indian Universities issues the equivalence certificate most colleges ask for; NEET and JEE Main each set their own rules on subjects and eligibility for students of other boards; CUET-UG opens the door to many central universities. Maharashtra's state-run engineering and pharmacy counselling, handled by the CET Cell, brings extra domicile and eligibility conditions that are best read in the current brochure.",
        "Applications abroad start a year or more before arrival. British universities want predicted IB points or A Level grades by the autumn of the last school year, and American colleges weigh those predictions beside essays and teacher references. Since a prediction leans on how a student performed in Class 11, the early months of a course matter more than families expect.",
        "What a tutor offers is academic: clear teaching, honest feedback on where a predicted grade could rise, and exam technique. Picking courses, drafting personal statements and filling in forms stay with the family and the school counsellor, and the tutor writes none of them.",
