        "Mathematics is where a State Board habit shows most. A Majalgaon student used to finishing a textbook sum in six lines meets an HL Analysis and Approaches question that runs for a page, offers no method and punishes a slip in line two all the way to the answer. The first fortnight of lessons goes on planning before calculating: what is asked, which tool fits, what a sensible answer looks like. Students who do this stop losing marks they had already earned.",
        "Applications and Interpretation is the better fit for a student who thinks in data. Its exploration needs a question the student can measure, and Beed district hands over plenty: reservoir levels across the year against rainfall, the weekly price of cotton or soybean at the market, water use on a family plot. A topic chosen in Class 11, not in the last month, leaves time to collect enough data and to revise the analysis once.",
        "In Physics and Chemistry, marks attach to reasons. A correct value without units, or quoted to absurd precision, loses credit, and a choice left unjustified counts for nothing. The laboratory component is judged on design and evaluation, which worries students whose schools have thin equipment; the answer is an investigation they can really perform, such as cooling curves, pendulum timing or a simple titration, planned and analysed carefully.",
        "Biology and Environmental Systems reward precise reading of the question. The verb decides the answer: describe wants what happens, explain wants why, evaluate wants a judgement backed by evidence. A tutor who lays a student's scripts beside the mark scheme can show the pattern within one session, and the repair is faster than more revision of content already known.",
